</figure>

But the unit also measures *A* according to the units in it. Therefore the unit is to *A* as *A* is to *B.* And, since *A* multiplied by *B* makes *C,* therefore *B* measures *C* according to the units in *A.* [!just VII.Def.20]

But the unit also measures *A* according to the units in it.

Therefore the unit is to *A* as *B* is to *C.* But the unit is to *A* as *A* is to *B,* therefore *A* is to *B* as *B* is to *C.* [!just VII.Def.20]

And, since *B* and *C* are cubes, therefore they are similar solid numbers.

Therefore there are two mean proportional numbers between *B* and *C.* And *B* is to *C* as *A* is to *B.* Therefore there are two mean proportional numbers between *A* and *B* also. [!just VIII.19; VIII.8]

And *B* is a cube, therefore *A* is also a cube. [!just VIII.23]

Therefore, *if a number multiplied by itself makes a cubic number, then it itself is also cubic.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

#### Outline of the proof

Assume that *a*<sup>2</sup> is a cube. Since *a*<sup>3</sup> is also a cube, therefore there are two mean proportionals between them (@VIII.19). But we have the proportion *a* : *a*<sup>2</sup> = *a*<sup>2</sup>:*a*<sup>3</sup>, so there are also two mean proportionals between *a* and *a*<sup>2</sup> (@VIII.8). And since *a*<sup>2</sup> is a cube, therefore *a* is also a cube (@VIII.23).

#### Use of this proposition

This proposition is used in @IX.10.
